# tools/gen/chapter/sunn8_root_readme_witness.rish -- SUNN8 root README living face.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/gen/chapter/sunn8_root_readme_witness.rish
#
# SUNN8 seated the front door; the standing voice molted Riyo -> Kyri on
# 20260810 (Keaton's word), so this witness tracks the LIVING voice. The
# 20260808 first-principles README rewrite dropped journey stamps, so the old
# "SUNN8" stamp assertion is retired -- the door names facts, not rung numbers.

say "sunn8: Language EN — the front door names the living pier."
say "sunn8: Style Radiant — Kyri · Livermore · xykj61 · SOURCE."
say "sunn8: Lens TAME — SUNN8 seat · voice/name/onboarding · Quin OS stays."

let r = read-file "README.md"
assert r contains "**Voice:** [Kyri](context/KYRI.md)" else "sunn8: want the front door to name Kyri as the voice, linked to her identity page"
# THE MAINTAINER'S NAME RETIRES FROM THIS ASSERTION (`20260908.183744`, Keaton's grant). `7191a938b`
# made ONE README serve BOTH repositories, and the public projection scrubs a personal name by design
# (`tools/fixtures/s/sow_scrub.sed`), so a front door REQUIRED to carry the name is a front door that
# can never be one page. The name stands where naming it is the point: `context/PUBKEYS.md` and the
# LICENSE files, which `MAP.md` holds until Keaton names a copyright rewrite. What this witness still
# asks of the front door is its VOICE -- restored on the line above after sixteen days away.
assert r contains "context/KYRI.md" else "sunn8: want the front door to link its voice page"
# The one-page front door names the PUBLIC route a newcomer can actually take; the private remote
# lives in `context/REMOTE_ROSTER.md`, which is where a maintainer looks for it.
assert r contains "SOURCE.md" else "sunn8: want the SOURCE onboarding door"
assert r contains "SOURCE.md" else "sunn8: want SOURCE.md onboarding"
assert r contains "context/KYRI.md" else "sunn8: want KYRI.md"
assert r contains "standing voice" else "sunn8: want the front door to name a standing voice"
# The restored line states the same fact in the page's own Bhakta register rather than in the elder
# sentence, so the assertion asks for the FACT -- the voice, named and linked -- rather than a wording.
assert r contains "[Kyri](context/KYRI.md)" else "sunn8: want the front door to seat Kyri as the standing writing voice in full"
assert (r contains "standing writing companion") == false else "sunn8: must not keep Quin as standing writing companion"
assert (r contains "standing voice **Quin**") == false else "sunn8: must not keep Quin as profile default voice"
assert (r contains "standing voice **Riyo**") == false else "sunn8: must not keep Riyo as living profile default"
# THE HAT MOVED TO ITS OWN HOME (`20260908.183744`). The front door is a Bhakta welcome for a reader
# meeting computing itself, and the fifth OS variant serves no newcomer standing there. The fact is
# held where a reader looks for it, so this assertion reads THAT page -- the hat stays guarded rather
# than merely relocated.
let var = read-file "manual/grain-os/variants/README.md"
assert var contains "fifth boot image, intentionally unpaired" else "sunn8: Quin OS hat must stay named in the variants page"
# The Q-vane hat was renamed **Lumen** on `20260816` (ITINERARY, seated names), so the
# front door naming `Q-vane` would now be naming a retired word. What this witness
# still holds is the hat that stayed: Quin the fifth boot image, asserted above.

say "GREEN: sunn8 — README Kyri · Livermore · xykj61 · SOURCE · Quin the fifth boot image held"
